<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Sudan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> is a historical term for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, from the Atlantic to around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. It was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> empires, whose wealth rested on gold and trans-Saharan trade. Accounts of the Songhai Empire note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a> was recognised by the Abbasid shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as viceroy of the western Sudan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-sudan.jpg" alt="Western Sudan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Timothy Akolamazima · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kob_ewes_Image02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In West Africa, the southern branch under Abu Bakr ibn Umar helped spread Maliki Islam through the Sahel, contributing to the Islamisation of the <b>western Sudan</b> and shaping the religious character of the later Mali and Songhai empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Military power was otherwise deployed primarily to enforce tributary relationships with subordinate kingdoms across the <b>western Sudan</b>, extracting regular payments that supplemented trade revenues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its dyed cloth was worn far beyond Hausaland, and its markets drew merchants from North Africa, Bornu and the <b>western Sudan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Askia Muhammad's pilgrimage to Mecca (1496–1498), during which the Abbasid shadow caliph in Cairo recognised him as his deputy in the <b>western Sudan</b>, gave the empire a religious legitimacy that Sunni Ali's syncretic rule had never achieved and inaugurated the empire's golden age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The clerical caste of the Ineslemen spread the faith across North Africa and the Sahel, and the Tuareg helped carry it into the <b>Western Sudan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
